                    </mxGeometry>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="61zq9GeNbJCBJ8Scp14T-22" connectable="0" parent="61zq9GeNbJCBJ8Scp14T-21" style="edgeLabel;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;resizable=0;points=[];" value="&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(180, 178, 169);&quot;&gt;Trebuchet Angle&lt;/span&gt;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry relative="1" x="0.1192" y="-12" as="geometry">
                        <mxPoint as="offset"/>
                    </mxGeometry>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="61zq9GeNbJCBJ8Scp14T-24" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;Hard Math&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;p dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;Let &lt;em&gt;d&lt;/em&gt; = baseline distance AB (measured once).&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;At the splash, Station A reads angle α, Station B reads angle β.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;Angle at splash: γ = 180° − α − β&lt;br&gt;Distance A→splash: AP = d · sin(β) / sin(γ)&lt;br&gt;Distance B→splash: BP = d · sin(α) / sin(γ)&lt;br&gt;Self-check: AB_check = √(AP² + BP² − 2·AP·BP·cos γ)&lt;br&gt;Splash coordinates: x = AP·cos(α), y = AP·sin(α)&lt;br&gt;Trebuchet→splash distance: √((x_splash − x_treb)² + (y_splash − y_treb)²)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="190" width="560" x="640" y="215" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="61zq9GeNbJCBJ8Scp14T-26" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;Plain-ish English&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;ol dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;&lt;li&gt;Measure the distance between your two watchers once. That&#39;s your ruler for everything else.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;li&gt;When the ball splashes, each watcher just points at it and reads one number off their sighting tool (the angle between &quot;the other watcher&quot; and &quot;the splash.&quot;)&lt;/li&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;li&gt;Those two angles tell you the shape of the triangle formed by the two watchers and the splash. A bigger or smaller angle stretches or shrinks that triangle, which is what turns your one measured distance into the actual distance to the splash.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;li&gt;You do this exact same trick once, ahead of time, to pin down where each trebuchet sits — same two watchers, same idea, just aimed at a fixed spot instead of a splash.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;li&gt;Now that you know exactly where the trebuchet is and exactly where the splash is, finding the distance between them is just like measuring between two dots on a map with a ruler.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="440" width="440" x="-436" y="20" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="61zq9GeNbJCBJ8Scp14T-27" parent="1" style="shape=umlActor;verticalLabelPosition=bottom;verticalAlign=top;html=1;outlineConnect=0;" value="Surveyor" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="40" width="20" x="80" y="260" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="61zq9GeNbJCBJ8Scp14T-28" parent="1" style="shape=umlActor;verticalLabelPosition=bottom;verticalAlign=top;html=1;outlineConnect=0;" value="Surveyor" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="40" width="20" x="560" y="260" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
